{"context":"The CLI ships as release binaries installed by `install.sh` / `install.ps1` (ADR-023; the release asset naming contract in `release.yml`). The update path has been **install-script-only**: re-running the installer is the documented way to move to a newer binary. Three problems follow:\n\n1. **Undiscoverable from an installed binary** — an already-installed `eka` has no command that tells the user a newer release exists or moves them to it; the update path lives in a script that shipped with a release the user may no longer have.\n2. **Silent drift** — old binaries do not announce their age; users run stale versions indefinitely, and version-dependent behavior (pack `requiresEka`, new commands, rule changes) diverges quietly.\n3. **No comparison surface** — the installed binary cannot answer \"is there a newer release?\" without fetching and running an installer script.\n\nSelf-update is now right for three reasons. The CLI already carries its build version (set at build time via `-ldflags`, surfaced by `eka version`), so **version comparison is trivial**. The release pipeline already publishes exactly what a self-update needs — `eka-\u003cos\u003e-\u003carch\u003e[.exe]` + `SHA256SUMS.txt` per release, the naming contract `install.sh`/`install.ps1` already resolve and verify — so **no new distribution infrastructure is required**. And the CLI's determinism contract (identical input → identical output; non-TTY output byte-identical, no ANSI control sequences) is a design driver, not an aspiration: the command must behave deterministically when piped, including its refusal paths.\n\nThis ADR explicitly amends ADR-023. ADR-023 rejected `eka update` for v1: *\"Standalone `eka uninstall` / `eka update` commands — rejected for v1: re-running `eka install` is the update\"* — that rejection addressed the **pack's** update path (re-running `eka install` refreshes the embedded pack) and left the binary's update to the install scripts. ADR-023's Consequences also flagged the risk that *\"users may expect `eka install` to update the binary itself (out of scope — `install.sh`/`install.ps1` remain the binary update path)\"*. That note is now **superseded**: the binary update path becomes `eka update`; `install.sh`/`install.ps1` remain the fresh-install path.\n\nLike `eka install`, the command is **distribution convenience, not runtime surface**: it never touches the EKA workspace or the canonical store.","decision":"1. **`eka update` is a new CLI command** — it self-updates the running binary in place: resolves the latest release of `maleolabs/engineering-knowledge-architecture` (GitHub API `/releases/latest`, the `tag_name`), downloads the matching release asset (`eka-\u003cos\u003e-\u003carch\u003e` for linux/darwin × amd64/arm64, `eka-\u003cos\u003e-\u003carch\u003e.exe` for windows), verifies its SHA-256 against the release's `SHA256SUMS.txt`, and atomically replaces the running binary. Distribution convenience, not runtime surface: never touches the EKA workspace or the canonical store (mirrors ADR-023). The implementation lives in `cmd/update.go`, with the download-progress UI component in `cmd/ui/downloadbar.go`; the command is registered in `cmd/root.go`.\n\n2. **Checksum verification is fail-closed.** A missing `SHA256SUMS.txt`, a missing asset entry, or a hash mismatch refuses — no binary is ever replaced unverified (the installer trust model, moved into the binary).\n\n3. **Atomic replace with `.old` recovery.** The new binary is swapped in via a rename dance; the prior binary is preserved as `\u003cbinary\u003e.old` for recovery. On Windows, replace failures surface as a deterministic refusal with the `install.sh` fallback hint.\n\n4. **Release resolution has a fallback.** Primary: the GitHub API (`GET /releases/latest`, `tag_name`). When the API is unavailable or rate-limited: a redirect probe — `HEAD /releases/latest/download/SHA256SUMS.txt`, the tag read from the `Location` header.\n\n5. **Flags: `--check` and `--yes`.** `--check` compares only — no download, no prompt; exit `0` when up to date, exit `1` when an update is available. `--yes` skips the TTY confirmation prompt; a non-TTY invocation without `--yes` refuses deterministically (exit `2`) — a piped run never blocks on an invisible prompt, and piped output carries no control sequences.\n\n6. **Version semantics.** A `version == \"dev\"` binary (built from source without `-ldflags`) cannot be compared: the command warns and treats the binary as always-outdated.\n\n7. **Exit codes.** `0` up to date or updated · `1` update available under `--check`, or refusal (network, checksum, platform, permissions, environment state) · `2` usage (non-TTY without `--yes`, bad flags).\n\n8. **UX follows the single-operation interaction model.** Context header + report + summary; the soft 256-color palette, `✓` `•` `→` `↓` icons, no emoji. Download progress uses the existing ProgressBar glyphs (`█` filled / `░` empty, width 10, ratio-colored), redrawn in place on a TTY at ~100ms throttle; non-TTY output is a deterministic single line — no control sequences when piped.","consequences":"- **Positive.** In-place self-update discoverable from the installed binary (`eka update`), closing silent drift; version comparison is trivial (the CLI carries its version via `-ldflags`); the existing release pipeline is reused exactly (the `release.yml` asset contract) — no new distribution infrastructure; CI-friendly (`--check` in pipelines, `--yes` non-interactive); ADR-023's \"users may expect `eka install` to update the binary\" risk note is resolved.\n- **Negative.** Requires network at update time (air-gapped installs keep the script or package path); the binary grows by the downloader surface; an interrupted replace can leave the `\u003cbinary\u003e.old` recovery file behind; the progress bar adds a TTY-only animation component (bounded by the determinism contract: piped output is one line).\n- **Risks.** GitHub API rate limits (mitigated: the redirect-based fallback); Windows self-replace is not always atomic (mitigated: deterministic refusal + `install.sh` fallback hint); users may confuse `eka update` (binary) with `eka install` (pack) — the two are deliberately separate concerns: after a CLI upgrade, re-run `eka install` to refresh the embedded pack.\n\n### Future Extensibility (not implemented)\n\n- **Version pinning** — `eka update --version \u003ctag\u003e` downloading a specific release (the `install.sh --version` analogue).\n- **Rollback** — `eka update --rollback` restoring the `.old` binary.\n- **Pre-release handling** — explicit channel selection (`--pre`), stable by default.\n- **Stronger verification** — signed checksums (cosign/minisign) over the current SHA-256 contract.\n- **Update notification** — a `--check`-style hint surfaced by `eka version` output.\n\n### References\n\n- [ADR-023](adr-023-skill-pack-install-command.md) — the command family this derives from; its v1 rejection of `eka update` and its \"users may expect `eka install` to update the binary\" risk note are superseded by this ADR\n- The installers — [`../../install.sh`](../../install.sh) (the fresh-install path; the fail-closed trust model this command inherits)\n- The release pipeline — [`../../.github/workflows/release.yml`](../../.github/workflows/release.yml) (the release asset naming contract `eka-\u003cos\u003e-\u003carch\u003e[.exe]` + `SHA256SUMS.txt`, the comment above the workflow)\n- The command — [`../../cmd/update.go`](../../cmd/update.go), the download-progress UI [`../../cmd/ui/downloadbar.go`](../../cmd/ui/downloadbar.go), registration [`../../cmd/root.go`](../../cmd/root.go)\n- ADR index: [`../adr-summary.md`](../adr-summary.md)","alternativesConsidered":"- **Install-script-only updates (status quo)** — rejected: undiscoverable from an already-installed binary; old binaries drift silently; no comparison surface without fetching and running a script.\n- **`eka install` updates the binary too** — rejected: pack distribution and binary distribution are different concerns; conflating them couples the pack's cadence to the binary's and surprises scripted installs.\n- **In-place overwrite of the running binary** — rejected: non-atomic; a failed write corrupts the only copy. The rename dance with `.old` recovery is the atomic pattern.\n- **Package-manager distribution (Homebrew and similar)** — rejected for this milestone: external infrastructure outside the repository's control; `eka update` serves the release-binary population the repository already ships to."}